On Monday, April 29, 2013 8:39:35 PM UTC+4, clifford wrote:
>
> Earlier this year, when I was working on criteria for a perfect loglang 
> with constant references to the existing specimens, one of the few flaws in 
> Lojban that emerged early in the study was the proliferation of 
> connectives.  For almost all the cases except the sentential ones (basic in 
> logic, whatever may be the case in Lojban) there were clearer ways of 
> handling the situation (some of them remarkably rare, by the way) than 
> using a new connective.  Some were even shorter.  To be sure, these 
> efficiencies were in a system being built from scratch, without the inertia 
> of 20 years of development, but most look to be adaptable to existing 
> Lojban with some inevitable backward loss.
>


And so what are they?

btw, another vote for the mad proposals is that  we actually have no word 
for AND in Lojban. Instead we have three words which is stupid IMO.


